-* Whether an idle P cluster keeps its last clock and voltage. schedutil
-  re-evaluates a policy only on scheduler events of its CPUs, so a
-  cluster that goes idle keeps the last pstate it asked for; whether the
-  hardware lowers the voltage by itself once every core is down is not
-  known. `scaling_cur_freq` reads the hardware status register
-  (`APPLE_DVFS_STATUS`), so sampling it in 200 ms windows in which a
-  cluster's CPUs had no busy jiffies answers it. On this busy machine no
-  P cluster was idle for a whole 200 ms window in 30 s, so it needs a quiet
-  one. If the clock stays up, part of the "PD always" saving is leakage at a
-  voltage nobody needs, and lowering the request when a cluster goes idle
-  would add to it.
-  m1n1 points to the hardware doing it: for T602x it resets every cluster
-  to its "APSC" pstate (pstate 1) and sets "APSC Snooze" (cluster base +
-  0x200f8, bit 40; `src/cpufreq.c` `cpufreq_init_cluster()`), which reads
-  as an automatic drop to that pstate when the cluster idles. For T8103 and
-  T8112 it also copies the APSC pstate's table entry into a separate slot
-  (cluster + 0x70210, and + 0x7ffe8 on T8112); for T602x it does not, so
-  whether snooze has a valid target there is a question for the m1n1
-  authors, or for a read of those registers from m1n1's proxy.
+* Answered later the same night: an idle P cluster does not keep its
+  clock. With the machine quieter, in 300 windows of 200 ms the P clusters
+  had no busy jiffies 29 times, and `scaling_cur_freq` (the hardware status
+  register, `APPLE_DVFS_STATUS`) read 702 MHz, the lowest pstate, in 28 of
+  them; the E cluster read 912 MHz in 6 of 7. That fits m1n1's "APSC
+  Snooze" setup (cluster base + 0x200f8, bit 40, with the cluster reset to
+  APSC pstate 1; `src/cpufreq.c` `cpufreq_init_cluster()`), though for
+  T602x m1n1 does not copy the snooze pstate into the slot it fills on
+  T8103/T8112. So the "PD always" saving is not leakage of whole idle
+  clusters at a high clock: it is the idle cores of busy clusters, which
+  sit at the voltage their busy siblings set, and that is why it grows with
+  load.
 
 * Whether iBoot arms the wake path (reconfig engine, boot CPU RVBAR) for a
   raw image as it does for a kernelcache.
